Short Report: Red Light Green Light Project—Developed as Web App with FastAPI

	Before I started this project in Web App with FastAPI, I had already developed this project in Python OpenCV. It works well, and I’m much more familiar with the Python environment, although I'm also familiar with HTML, CSS, and JS, but my knowledge using these foundational tools was not that deep. 

	The algorithm used for this project is based on frame difference motion detection. For every frame captured from the webcam, it is actually processed, following these steps: Frame capturing → Frame resizing to a fixed width → Converting to grayscale → Applying Gaussian blur → Calculating frame absolute difference from previous and current frame → Computing motion score → Comparing the motion score from the given threshold. After comparing the motion score and given threshold, the result of this will be the basis for what will be the “State” of the program. 

	This RLGL game project operates using machine states such as start (the initial state before the game begins), green (the player should make a move), warning (when the player doesn't move in the green state), red (the player should stay still), and dead (the player failed the rule). The state transitions as follows: 

START → GREEN (game starts)

GREEN → RED (randomized time between 2.6 and 4.2 sec)

GREEN → WARNING (player is not making a move)

WARNING → DEAD (player is staying still)

RED → DEAD (player is moving while in red state)

RED → GREEN (timer expires)

The parameters that I used in this project were based on the suggested baseline parameters in our PDF file for this project. There is no adjustment made when using those parameters. 

