While doing this game project, one of the failures I encountered was when I was trying to adapt my Python code to a web application. Because OpenCV normally runs in a Python local environment application or window, I seek assistance and use LLMs such as ChatGPT to understand first how I can run or adapt my Python code to view or access the webcam using the web browser; however, before I can access the webcam in my browser, I need to understand how FastAPI works. This is the second failure I need to fix. I need to understand how to use FastAPI endpoints. It was hard to understand at first how a Python script acts like a web server and talks to a browser's interface. What I did at that moment was to experiment with sample codes to see how FastAPI provides decorators for all standard HTTP methods to perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations. The next failure I encountered was  synchronizing the game state between the backend and frontend. The solution to this is to use setInterval() (a JavaScript timer function) that executes code such as “fetch” at a fixed time interval.

In summary, this project shows how computer vision and web applications can work together to make a motion detection game that we can play. OpenCV is used to process images, FastAPI is used for backend services, and a web dashboard is used for the user interface or frontend. Even though the system can sense motion and change its state, working on it required me to learn a lot of new technologies, such as FastAPI, API endpoints, and browser communication.
